  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCool northwest flow pattern today with lake effect rain showers\nand gusty winds continuing through this evening. Ridge of high\npressure will start to build in overnight with much improved\nconditions for Tuesday. Expect decreasing clouds Tuesday across\nmuch of the area, except for possibly the NYS Thruway corridor\non northward. Highs should top out in the upper 50s to low 60s\nfor most of the region. Wednesday should remain mostly dry, but\nan incoming clipper system could start spreading rain showers\ninto the region by Wednesday evening. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe pattern will turn much more active for the end of the week\nand into the weekend. The guidance is in agreement that a large\nupper trough settles over the Northeast U.S. Thursday into\nFriday. However, some model guidance and ensembles try to\ndevelop low pressure off the southern coast of New England or\nLong Island. There remains quite a bit of spread on where/if\nthis system will develop and track. What does appear more likely\nat this time is our region will be at least under the influence\nof an upper level trough/low and cooler temperatures are likely\nfor the end of the week and this weekend. As for potential\nsnowfall, boundary layer temperatures at this time appear fairly\nmarginal, with the best chance for any snow accumulations to\noccur during the overnight hours Friday into Saturday and most\nlikely in the higher elevation areas. \n\nThis upper level low could persist through the weekend and into\nthe first part of next week. This would keep conditions cool and\nunsettled under northwest flow, with lake effect rain/snow \nshowers possible.",
